WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.269
Welcome to the story deck for
Building Provider Notices,

2
00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:06.763
a developer workbook in the
Federal Modernization Workbook.

3
00:00:06.763 --> 00:00:09.468
Instead of eight chapters one at a time,

4
00:00:09.468 --> 00:00:14.118
we'll follow one notice all the way
through: an Azure Service Health

5
00:00:14.118 --> 00:00:19.613
retirement of basic public IP addresses,
action required by September 30, 2027.

6
00:00:19.613 --> 00:00:23.734
It's fictional: every value comes
from the kit's own test fixtures,

7
00:00:23.734 --> 00:00:27.388
and every output on a slide is
what the kit really printed.

8
00:00:27.388 --> 00:00:28.962
Two notes before we begin.

9
00:00:28.962 --> 00:00:32.312
The workbook is a draft,
awaiting the owner's review.

10
00:00:32.312 --> 00:00:37.980
And the code is illustrative, tested
against fixtures, not against a live tenant.

11
00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:42.718
Here's the route.

12
00:00:42.718 --> 00:00:46.192
Scene one: Azure Service
Health reports the retirement,

13
00:00:46.192 --> 00:00:48.080
and the kit makes it one record.

14
00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:54.585
Scenes two to five ask the same question
of Microsoft 365 in GCC, Oracle Cloud, AWS

15
00:00:54.585 --> 00:01:00.992
and Google Cloud: how does each provider
say something needs action, and by when?

16
00:01:00.992 --> 00:01:04.407
Those scenes use each
provider's own fixture notices,

17
00:01:04.407 --> 00:01:06.580
because our retirement is Azure's.

18
00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:10.518
Scene six gives the notice an
owner, as a Ticket-to-Machine task.

19
00:01:10.518 --> 00:01:14.792
Scene seven carries it into the
weekly digest and the evidence chain.

20
00:01:14.792 --> 00:01:19.142
And scene eight deploys the whole
loop, test first, then production.

21
00:01:19.142 --> 00:01:20.994
Each scene has a story slide,

22
00:01:20.994 --> 00:01:24.990
a try-it slide with real output,
and a one-question check.

23
00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:29.565
Scene one.

24
00:01:29.565 --> 00:01:33.407
Microsoft Learn's Resource Graph
samples for Service Health show

25
00:01:33.407 --> 00:01:37.040
a retirement as a health advisory
with the subtype retirement.

26
00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:38.778
That's all Microsoft says.

27
00:01:38.778 --> 00:01:41.353
Nothing in the event
says action is required.

28
00:01:41.353 --> 00:01:43.239
So the kit does three things.

29
00:01:43.239 --> 00:01:47.690
It merges the event's rows from
every subscription into one notice.

30
00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:49.421
It applies a stated rule:

31
00:01:49.421 --> 00:01:53.490
a retirement is action required,
due at the advisory's end.

32
00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:57.378
And it writes who said so,
the kit's rule, not Microsoft.

33
00:01:57.378 --> 00:02:01.915
Then it fingerprints the record, so
reading it again next week changes nothing.

34
00:02:01.915 --> 00:02:06.296
Why it matters: monitoring the
provider, and receiving its advisories,

35
00:02:06.296 --> 00:02:09.450
with evidence the security
officer can keep.

36
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:13.925
Try it.

37
00:02:13.925 --> 00:02:17.537
From the kit's folder, collect
Azure from the day-one fixtures.

38
00:02:17.537 --> 00:02:22.001
Five notices, all valid against
the schema, two needing action.

39
00:02:22.001 --> 00:02:24.690
Five from six rows, because one incident's

40
00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:27.775
rows from two subscriptions
became one notice.

41
00:02:27.775 --> 00:02:31.469
Read the records, and
there's our retirement:

42
00:02:31.469 --> 00:02:35.163
open, action required,
due September 30, 2027.

43
00:02:35.163 --> 00:02:36.474
Then read it in full.

44
00:02:36.474 --> 00:02:40.065
The window runs a year, the
due date is the window's end,

45
00:02:40.065 --> 00:02:45.325
and the action basis says rule, Azure
retirement, due at the advisory end time.

46
00:02:45.325 --> 00:02:49.378
The source link is the event on the
commercial Resource Manager host,

47
00:02:49.378 --> 00:02:52.090
and the fingerprint is the record's hash.

48
00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:56.652
Check one.

49
00:02:56.652 --> 00:03:02.278
The record says our retirement is
action required by September 30, 2027.

50
00:03:02.278 --> 00:03:03.465
Who decided that?

51
00:03:03.465 --> 00:03:05.840
Microsoft, in a field on the event?

52
00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:07.215
The kit's stated rule?

53
00:03:07.215 --> 00:03:09.677
The security officer, reviewing the run?

54
00:03:09.677 --> 00:03:12.365
Or ServiceNow, when the task was created?

55
00:03:12.365 --> 00:03:14.790
The answer opens the next scene.

56
00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:21.228
The answer to check one:
the kit's stated rule.

57
00:03:21.228 --> 00:03:24.515
Microsoft's Service Health
event has no field for it.

58
00:03:24.515 --> 00:03:28.665
Scene two asks the same
question of Microsoft 365.

59
00:03:28.665 --> 00:03:30.453
Here Microsoft does say it.

60
00:03:30.453 --> 00:03:34.514
Microsoft Learn's service
communications API gives a Message

61
00:03:34.514 --> 00:03:38.028
center post an action-required-by
date, Microsoft's own.

62
00:03:38.028 --> 00:03:42.046
The fixtures hold a
Microsoft 365 retirement too:

63
00:03:42.046 --> 00:03:46.553
Basic authentication for
SMTP AUTH client submission.

64
00:03:46.553 --> 00:03:50.677
It has that date, so its action
basis says provider, not rule.

65
00:03:50.677 --> 00:03:55.965
The workbook's tenant is GCC, which
reads the worldwide Graph host.

66
00:03:55.965 --> 00:03:58.708
GCC High and DoD have their own hosts,

67
00:03:58.708 --> 00:04:02.450
and the kit refuses a
session in the wrong one.

68
00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:06.925
Try it.

69
00:04:06.925 --> 00:04:09.582
First, the hosts the kit knows:

70
00:04:09.582 --> 00:04:15.450
GCC on the worldwide Graph endpoint,
GCC High and DoD on their own.

71
00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:20.538
Then collect Microsoft 365 from
the fixtures and read the records.

72
00:04:20.538 --> 00:04:27.225
The SMTP AUTH retirement is action
required by January 31, 2027.

73
00:04:27.225 --> 00:04:29.875
Then read each notice's action basis.

74
00:04:29.875 --> 00:04:35.388
The retirement's says provider, action
required by date time: Microsoft's own field.

75
00:04:35.388 --> 00:04:38.028
The SharePoint Framework post says rule:

76
00:04:38.028 --> 00:04:42.375
a prevent-or-fix post with no date is
action required by the kit's rule.

77
00:04:42.375 --> 00:04:44.934
Same record shape as our Azure retirement,

78
00:04:44.934 --> 00:04:48.070
with the source of each
decision written down.

79
00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:52.645
Check two.

80
00:04:52.645 --> 00:04:56.920
A GCC High tenant's settings
say G C C by mistake.

81
00:04:56.920 --> 00:04:59.683
Without the kit's guard, what
would the collection do?

82
00:04:59.683 --> 00:05:02.094
Fail loudly, read the posts anyway,

83
00:05:02.094 --> 00:05:07.290
return nothing and pass on an empty
list, or read someone else's posts?

84
00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:13.862
The answer to check two:
it would return nothing,

85
00:05:13.862 --> 00:05:16.178
and the gate would pass on an empty list.

86
00:05:16.178 --> 00:05:18.503
That's why the cloud is always named.

87
00:05:18.503 --> 00:05:21.673
Scene three, Oracle Cloud Infrastructure,

88
00:05:21.673 --> 00:05:24.753
where an agency's HR
platform may well run.

89
00:05:24.753 --> 00:05:27.995
Oracle's documentation gives
the announcement a type,

90
00:05:27.995 --> 00:05:30.590
and that type can itself
be action required.

91
00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:33.927
That's Oracle's own flag,
so no kit rule is needed.

92
00:05:33.927 --> 00:05:36.495
The fixtures' example is a change:

93
00:05:36.495 --> 00:05:42.103
replace the certificate bundle on load
balancers, due December 15, 2026.

94
00:05:42.103 --> 00:05:45.396
The kit reads the latest
announcement in each chain,

95
00:05:45.396 --> 00:05:48.153
so a rescheduled window
replaces the first.

96
00:05:48.153 --> 00:05:50.746
And it knows Oracle's realms: commercial,

97
00:05:50.746 --> 00:05:54.250
and the US Government
realm with its own regions.

98
00:05:58.000 --> 00:05:58.725
Try it.

99
00:05:58.725 --> 00:06:01.050
First, the realms the kit knows.

100
00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:05.400
The commercial realm, and the US
Government realm with its two regions.

101
00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:09.913
The third realm is refused, with the
reason: Oracle's page for its endpoint

102
00:06:09.913 --> 00:06:13.975
wasn't readable when the chapter
was written, so the kit won't guess.

103
00:06:13.975 --> 00:06:16.800
Then read the day-one OCI records.

104
00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:21.946
A database maintenance window that names
a database system, the certificate bundle

105
00:06:21.946 --> 00:06:26.725
change that's action required by December
15, an advisory and a closed incident.

106
00:06:26.725 --> 00:06:31.060
Same record, same fields, from
a fourth provider's shape.

107
00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:35.722
Check three.

108
00:06:35.722 --> 00:06:38.455
Oracle reschedules that
database maintenance,

109
00:06:38.455 --> 00:06:41.323
and publishes a new
announcement in the same chain.

110
00:06:41.323 --> 00:06:45.865
What does the kit hold afterwards: two
notices, one with the latest window,

111
00:06:45.865 --> 00:06:49.630
one with the original window,
or nothing until it's over?

112
00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:57.305
The answer to check three: one
notice, with the latest window.

113
00:06:57.305 --> 00:06:59.430
Scene four, AWS.

114
00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:03.252
AWS's documentation says AWS
Health tells each account

115
00:07:03.252 --> 00:07:06.492
which of its instances
will be retired, and when.

116
00:07:06.492 --> 00:07:09.040
AWS has its own actionability field,

117
00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:13.117
and when it says action required,
that's the provider's word.

118
00:07:13.117 --> 00:07:18.064
But a scheduled change that names a retirement
or lifecycle event without that flag

119
00:07:18.064 --> 00:07:20.749
could look optional, so
the kit makes it action

120
00:07:20.749 --> 00:07:23.081
required by its own
rule, due at its start.

121
00:07:23.081 --> 00:07:27.236
Two things to know: the Health API
needs a qualifying support plan,

122
00:07:27.236 --> 00:07:30.392
and the GovCloud partition
has a single endpoint.

123
00:07:30.392 --> 00:07:34.050
The kit names the partition,
and checks the caller is in it.

124
00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:38.525
Try it.

125
00:07:38.525 --> 00:07:42.754
First, the partitions: commercial
AWS in its Health region,

126
00:07:42.754 --> 00:07:47.600
and GovCloud in its single West
region, each with its own ARN prefix.

127
00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:52.450
Then, after collecting the fixtures,
read each notice's action basis.

128
00:07:52.450 --> 00:07:57.772
The EC2 instance retirement and the
security notification say provider,

129
00:07:57.772 --> 00:08:00.913
actionability action
required: AWS said so.

130
00:08:00.913 --> 00:08:03.690
The Lambda planned
lifecycle event says rule:

131
00:08:03.690 --> 00:08:08.001
a scheduled change whose event type
names a retirement, due at its start.

132
00:08:08.001 --> 00:08:11.125
Maintenance and the closed
incident need no action.

133
00:08:11.125 --> 00:08:16.280
It's the same split our Azure retirement
showed: who said it is always written down.

134
00:08:20.030 --> 00:08:20.942
Check four.

135
00:08:20.942 --> 00:08:24.368
An AWS account has no
qualifying support plan.

136
00:08:24.368 --> 00:08:27.893
What does the Health API answer,
and what should the agency do?

137
00:08:27.893 --> 00:08:31.492
An empty list, the support
plan error and a recorded gap,

138
00:08:31.492 --> 00:08:35.060
a public status feed, or
another account's events?

139
00:08:38.810 --> 00:08:43.478
The answer to check four: AWS answers
with a subscription required error,

140
00:08:43.478 --> 00:08:47.135
and the agency records the
gap with the security officer.

141
00:08:47.135 --> 00:08:49.097
Scene five, Google Cloud.

142
00:08:49.097 --> 00:08:52.312
Google's documentation
for Personalized Service

143
00:08:52.312 --> 00:08:55.373
Health says its event has
one category: incident.

144
00:08:55.373 --> 00:08:57.148
Planned maintenance isn't in it.

145
00:08:57.148 --> 00:09:01.872
So a notice like our retirement can never
arrive from Google through this interface.

146
00:09:01.872 --> 00:09:04.011
The kit doesn't pretend otherwise.

147
00:09:04.011 --> 00:09:08.541
Every Google notice is an incident,
and the gap is written into the guide,

148
00:09:08.541 --> 00:09:11.460
the chapter and the
security officer's record.

149
00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:15.097
One more gap: Google lists no
Personalized Service Health

150
00:09:15.097 --> 00:09:19.240
in its Assured Workloads FedRAMP
and impact-level packages.

151
00:09:22.990 --> 00:09:23.715
Try it.

152
00:09:23.715 --> 00:09:27.558
Collect Google from the
day-one fixtures: two notices,

153
00:09:27.558 --> 00:09:30.790
one open, none needing
action, both incidents.

154
00:09:30.790 --> 00:09:34.228
Read them: a Cloud SQL
connection problem and a Cloud

155
00:09:34.228 --> 00:09:37.815
Storage latency problem, each
with its product and region.

156
00:09:37.815 --> 00:09:40.015
Then group both weeks by kind.

157
00:09:40.015 --> 00:09:42.890
Four records, and every
one is an incident.

158
00:09:42.890 --> 00:09:46.390
That's not a fault in the kit;
it's what the interface carries.

159
00:09:46.390 --> 00:09:49.637
So when the digest shows
Google with no retirements,

160
00:09:49.637 --> 00:09:54.220
that's a gap the security officer
has already recorded, not good news.

161
00:09:57.970 --> 00:09:58.908
Check five.

162
00:09:58.908 --> 00:10:03.883
The weekly digest shows Google Cloud
with no retirements and no maintenance.

163
00:10:03.883 --> 00:10:07.802
What does that tell the security
officer: Google has nothing planned,

164
00:10:07.802 --> 00:10:10.620
the reader lacks a role,
nothing at all because the

165
00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:14.420
interface carries only incidents,
or the collection failed?

166
00:10:18.170 --> 00:10:20.420
The answer to check five: nothing.

167
00:10:20.420 --> 00:10:25.608
Google's interface carries only incidents,
and that gap is already on record.

168
00:10:25.608 --> 00:10:28.207
Scene six brings us
back to our retirement.

169
00:10:28.207 --> 00:10:31.970
The router matches it against
the agency's routing file,

170
00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:34.096
rules in order, first match wins.

171
00:10:34.096 --> 00:10:36.629
It becomes a typed task: priority three,

172
00:10:36.629 --> 00:10:41.698
assigned to the cloud platform engineering
group, with a contingency review,

173
00:10:41.698 --> 00:10:46.370
because a retirement that names the
agency's resources is a plan change.

174
00:10:46.370 --> 00:10:49.964
Two dates stay apart: the
agency's response window,

175
00:10:49.964 --> 00:10:53.470
a day, and Microsoft's
action date, next September.

176
00:10:53.470 --> 00:10:58.664
The task follows the Ticket-to-Machine
pattern from the book Governance, Drift and

177
00:10:58.664 --> 00:11:04.140
Evidence: a human-decision lane and five
clauses, from origin to the evidence kept.

178
00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:08.615
Try it.

179
00:11:08.615 --> 00:11:12.615
After routing the day-one collection,
read our retirement's payload.

180
00:11:12.615 --> 00:11:19.001
Create, de-duplication key azure and the
tracking ID, P3 with a twenty-four-hour

181
00:11:19.001 --> 00:11:24.090
window, response due the next day,
action due September 30, 2027.

182
00:11:24.090 --> 00:11:27.504
Cloud Platform Engineering,
contingency review true,

183
00:11:27.504 --> 00:11:30.690
and the controls and
indicators the step supports.

184
00:11:30.690 --> 00:11:35.016
Then the five clauses: the human
decision lane, the notice as origin,

185
00:11:35.016 --> 00:11:39.342
the system owner as approver, plan
the change under change control,

186
00:11:39.342 --> 00:11:41.702
a re-read check, and the evidence kept.

187
00:11:41.702 --> 00:11:45.627
Finally, route the same collection
again on a copy of the state.

188
00:11:45.627 --> 00:11:48.960
Twenty-two unchanged, zero payloads.

189
00:11:52.710 --> 00:11:53.735
Check six.

190
00:11:53.735 --> 00:11:57.510
Next spring, Microsoft stops
listing our retirement notice.

191
00:11:57.510 --> 00:12:01.261
What does the router do with
its task: resolve it, delete it,

192
00:12:01.261 --> 00:12:05.780
send an update asking for the
re-read, or wait for the action date?

193
00:12:09.530 --> 00:12:13.117
The answer to check six: an
update asking for the re-read.

194
00:12:13.117 --> 00:12:17.068
A provider closing its notice
isn't the agency's work being done.

195
00:12:17.068 --> 00:12:18.118
Scene seven.

196
00:12:18.118 --> 00:12:22.729
The digest is for the people who act, and
the evidence is for the people who check.

197
00:12:22.729 --> 00:12:27.005
Both come from the same runs, so neither
can drift from what was actually read.

198
00:12:27.005 --> 00:12:31.847
Every week, our retirement sits in
the digest's action-required section,

199
00:12:31.847 --> 00:12:35.130
soonest due first, with
the group it was routed to.

200
00:12:35.130 --> 00:12:40.427
And every run is recorded as evidence:
its steps, the controls and indicators

201
00:12:40.427 --> 00:12:44.968
they support, every file's hash, and
the hash of the record before it.

202
00:12:44.968 --> 00:12:48.160
Change a recorded file,
and the check fails.

203
00:12:51.910 --> 00:12:52.635
Try it.

204
00:12:52.635 --> 00:12:56.835
After a week of runs, write the digest
for the second week and read it.

205
00:12:56.835 --> 00:13:02.240
Under action required, our row:
due 2027-09-30, Azure, retirement,

206
00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:07.222
routed to Cloud Platform
Engineering, with its notice ID.

207
00:13:07.222 --> 00:13:12.288
Then, with the collection, the routing
and the digest each recorded, check the

208
00:13:12.288 --> 00:13:17.273
evidence: twenty-five files match their
sums, three records, chain unbroken.

209
00:13:17.273 --> 00:13:21.023
Now append one line to the
recorded digest and check again.

210
00:13:21.023 --> 00:13:24.375
The sums fail on that file,
the chain fails on its record,

211
00:13:24.375 --> 00:13:26.820
and the check exits with a failure.

212
00:13:30.570 --> 00:13:31.508
Check seven.

213
00:13:31.508 --> 00:13:35.254
In the lab, the stage check
for test passes the sums,

214
00:13:35.254 --> 00:13:37.721
the chain, routing and the digest.

215
00:13:37.721 --> 00:13:39.070
Why does it still fail?

216
00:13:39.070 --> 00:13:43.255
An edited file, no pre-flight
or scheduled-run record yet,

217
00:13:43.255 --> 00:13:46.610
mismatched indicators, or an old digest?

218
00:13:50.360 --> 00:13:53.205
The answer to check seven:
there's no pre-flight or

219
00:13:53.205 --> 00:13:56.548
scheduled-run record yet, and
only the runbook makes them.

220
00:13:56.548 --> 00:13:58.322
Scene eight, the runbook.

221
00:13:58.322 --> 00:14:00.989
It's one ordered set
of commands run twice,

222
00:14:00.989 --> 00:14:05.460
against test and then production,
and only the settings file changes.

223
00:14:05.460 --> 00:14:07.933
Reads come first, one provider at a time,

224
00:14:07.933 --> 00:14:11.410
each cross-checked against
the provider's own command.

225
00:14:11.410 --> 00:14:15.955
Routing runs as a dry run, then a smoke
test, then the one write to a cloud,

226
00:14:15.955 --> 00:14:19.943
the Service Health alert policy
Microsoft Learn describes,

227
00:14:19.943 --> 00:14:22.335
applied only with its approved hash.

228
00:14:22.335 --> 00:14:24.981
Then the weekly schedule,
the stage's evidence,

229
00:14:24.981 --> 00:14:27.210
and a rollback someone has already run.

230
00:14:27.210 --> 00:14:32.300
One honest label: nobody has run this
runbook against a live tenant yet.

231
00:14:36.050 --> 00:14:36.775
Try it.

232
00:14:36.775 --> 00:14:39.417
The runbook's own dry run on the fixtures:

233
00:14:39.417 --> 00:14:44.861
the test pre-flight passes fourteen checks
and is recorded, then the scheduled run

234
00:14:44.861 --> 00:14:50.225
collects three providers, routes eight
creates as a dry run, and writes the digest.

235
00:14:50.225 --> 00:14:54.212
Now the stage check that failed in
the last scene passes on every row,

236
00:14:54.212 --> 00:14:56.988
because the pre-flight
and the run are recorded.

237
00:14:56.988 --> 00:15:01.063
The Azure step on the test fixture
shows one retirement, ours.

238
00:15:01.063 --> 00:15:05.297
And the production alert plan prints
the same hash as the chapter one lab,

239
00:15:05.297 --> 00:15:08.840
which a reviewer approves
before anything is applied.

240
00:15:12.590 --> 00:15:14.315
Check eight, the last one.

241
00:15:14.315 --> 00:15:19.203
An operator runs the production pre-flight
before the test stage's evidence passes.

242
00:15:19.203 --> 00:15:23.373
Does it pass anyway, fail on the
check that reads the test evidence,

243
00:15:23.373 --> 00:15:28.220
warn and let the operator decide, or
apply the alert policy regardless?

244
00:15:31.970 --> 00:15:35.493
The answer to check eight: the
pre-flight's last check fails,

245
00:15:35.493 --> 00:15:39.457
because production needs the test
stage's evidence to pass first.

246
00:15:39.457 --> 00:15:43.340
That's our retirement, end to end:
read with the least privilege,

247
00:15:43.340 --> 00:15:46.690
made one record that says
who decided it needs action,

248
00:15:46.690 --> 00:15:51.259
routed to an owner with two dates kept
apart, carried in the weekly digest,

249
00:15:51.259 --> 00:15:56.132
recorded as evidence the security officer
can check, and deployed test first.

250
00:15:56.132 --> 00:15:57.269
Now it's your turn.

251
00:15:57.269 --> 00:16:02.011
Each provider chapter has a second lab
in your own test subscription, tenant,

252
00:16:02.011 --> 00:16:05.096
tenancy, account or project,
and routing has one in

253
00:16:05.096 --> 00:16:08.332
a ServiceNow developer
instance, never production.

254
00:16:08.332 --> 00:16:12.422
Then go deeper: the workbook's
chapters and their narrated tours,

255
00:16:12.422 --> 00:16:15.508
the learning path, and
the implementation guide.

256
00:16:15.508 --> 00:16:16.660
Thanks for watching.
